§ `Chapters 43 to 48 -- escheats, officers, abbeys and the forest`

§§ `Chapter 43 -- the heir of one who holds of an escheat`

-- The amounts are in whatever unit the relief is paid in; the text does not state one.
-- src:93 | If one who holds of some escheat (such as the honour of Wallingford, of Nottingham, of Boulogne, of Lancaster, or of other escheats which are in our hands and are baronies) shall die, his heir shall give no other relief, and perform no other service to us than he would have done to the baron, if that barony had been in the baron’s hand;
@ref Chapter 43 clause 43.1 (src:93)
GIVEN `the deceased held of an escheat which is in the King's hands and is a barony` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the relief the King would take from the heir` IS A NUMBER
      `the relief the heir would have given the baron if the barony had been in the baron's hand` IS A NUMBER
      `the King would require a service the baron would not have had` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Bar
DECIDE `ch 43 -- the King may take this relief and service from the heir` IS
    IF `the deceased held of an escheat which is in the King's hands and is a barony`
    THEN IF   `the relief the King would take from the heir` GREATER THAN `the relief the heir would have given the baron if the barony had been in the baron's hand`
           OR `the King would require a service the baron would not have had`
         THEN Barred
         ELSE IF `the relief the King would take from the heir` EQUALS `the relief the heir would have given the baron if the barony had been in the baron's hand`
              -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "his heir shall give no other relief, and perform no other service to us than he would have done to the baron" (the list is closed by "no other ... than").
              THEN `Not barred`
              -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: if because "if" makes a comparison with the baron's hand and is met; whether a smaller relief is "other" is a reading (F43, F64c)).
              ELSE `Bar not covered`
    -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: if because "if" makes a comparison with the baron's hand; the branch is reached by a deceased who held of no such escheat, whom the sentence does not name).
    ELSE `Bar not covered`

-- src:93 | If one who holds of some escheat (such as the honour of Wallingford, of Nottingham, of Boulogne, of Lancaster, or of other escheats which are in our hands and are baronies) shall die, his heir shall give no other relief, and perform no other service to us than he would have done to the baron, if that barony had been in the baron’s hand;
@ref Chapter 43 clause 43.1 (src:93)
GIVEN `the deceased held of an escheat which is in the King's hands and is a barony` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the relief the King would take from the heir` IS A NUMBER
      `the relief the heir would have given the baron if the barony had been in the baron's hand` IS A NUMBER
      `the King would require a service the baron would not have had` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
DECIDE `ch 43 -- his heir shall give no other relief and perform no other service to us than he would have done to the baron` IS
    IF `ch 43 -- the King may take this relief and service from the heir` `the deceased held of an escheat which is in the King's hands and is a barony` `the relief the King would take from the heir` `the relief the heir would have given the baron if the barony had been in the baron's hand` `the King would require a service the baron would not have had` EQUALS Barred
    THEN PARTY `the King`
         SHANT `take from the heir of a holder of an escheat a relief or service greater than the baron would have had`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BY `the King` BECAUSE "ch 43: the King took from the heir of a holder of an escheat a relief or service greater than the baron would have had"
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- src:93 | and we shall hold it in the same manner in which the baron held it.
@ref Chapter 43 clause 43.2 (src:93)
GIVEN `the escheat is in the King's hands and is a barony` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN Obligation
DECIDE `ch 43 -- the King holds the escheat in the manner in which the baron held it` IS
    IF `the escheat is in the King's hands and is a barony`
    THEN Obliged
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text does not speak to a case outside the facts this rule names.
    ELSE `Obligation not covered`

-- src:93 | and we shall hold it in the same manner in which the baron held it.
@ref Chapter 43 clause 43.2 (src:93)
GIVEN `the escheat is in the King's hands and is a barony` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
DECIDE `ch 43 -- we shall hold it in the same manner in which the baron held it` IS
    IF `ch 43 -- the King holds the escheat in the manner in which the baron held it` `the escheat is in the King's hands and is a barony` EQUALS Obliged
    THEN PARTY `the King`
         MUST `hold the escheat in the same manner in which the baron held it`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BY `the King` BECAUSE "ch 43: the King did not hold the escheat in the manner in which the baron held it"
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§ `Chapter 44 -- the forest courts`

-- "need not ... except those who are impleaded, or who have become sureties": the exception
-- takes away the exemption; it does not itself say that they must come, so those men get
-- `Obligation not covered`.
-- src:95 | Men who dwell without the forest need not henceforth come before our justiciars of the forest upon a general summons, except those who are impleaded, or who have become sureties for any person or persons attached for forest offences.
@ref Chapter 44 (src:95)
GIVEN `the man dwells without the forest` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the man is impleaded` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the man has become surety for a person attached for forest offences` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN Obligation
DECIDE `ch 44 -- the man must come before the justiciars of the forest upon a general summons` IS
    IF `the man dwells without the forest`
    THEN IF   `the man is impleaded`
           OR `the man has become surety for a person attached for forest offences`
         -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: except because "except" only lifts the exemption "need not"; the sentence does not itself say those men must come (F44)).
         THEN `Obligation not covered`
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "need not henceforth come before our justiciars of the forest upon a general summons".
         ELSE `Not obliged`
    -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: except because "except" qualifies men who dwell without the forest; the branch is reached by a man who dwells within it).
    ELSE `Obligation not covered`

§§ `Chapter 45 -- the appointment of officers`

-- src:97 | We will appoint as justices, constables, sheriffs, or bailiffs only such as know the law of the realm and mean to observe it well.
@ref Chapter 45 (src:97)
GIVEN office IS AN OfficeKind
      `the person knows the law of the realm` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person means to observe it well` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Bar
DECIDE `ch 45 -- the King may appoint this person to the office` IS
    IF office EQUALS `some other office`
    -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: only because "only" closes the persons appointed (such as know the law), not the list of offices; the branch is reached by an office the text does not name).
    THEN `Bar not covered`
    ELSE IF   `the person knows the law of the realm`
          AND `the person means to observe it well`
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "only such as know the law of the realm and mean to observe it well".
         THEN `Not barred`
         ELSE Barred

-- src:97 | We will appoint as justices, constables, sheriffs, or bailiffs only such as know the law of the realm and mean to observe it well.
@ref Chapter 45 (src:97)
GIVEN office IS AN OfficeKind
      `the person knows the law of the realm` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person means to observe it well` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC Party Act
DECIDE `ch 45 -- we will appoint as justices, constables, sheriffs or bailiffs only such as know the law of the realm and mean to observe it well` IS
    IF `ch 45 -- the King may appoint this person to the office` office `the person knows the law of the realm` `the person means to observe it well` EQUALS Barred
    THEN PARTY `the King`
         SHANT `appoint as a justice, constable, sheriff or bailiff one who does not know the law of the realm and mean to observe it well`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST BREACH BY `the King` BECAUSE "ch 45: the King appointed as a justice, constable, sheriff or bailiff one who did not both know the law of the realm and mean to observe it well"
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§ `Chapter 46 -- abbeys founded by barons`

-- The text names no one who owes the wardship (fork F46).  "as they ought to have" is read as
-- a statement that the right is theirs, and takes no input.
-- src:99 | All barons who have founded abbeys, concerning which they hold charters from the kings of England, or of which they have long-continued possession, shall have the wardship of them, when vacant, as they ought to have.
@ref Chapter 46 (src:99)
GIVEN `the person is a baron` IS A BOOLEAN
      `he founded the abbey` IS A BOOLEAN
      `he holds charters from the kings of England concerning the abbey` IS A BOOLEAN
      `he has long-continued possession of the abbey` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the abbey is vacant` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN Entitlement
DECIDE `ch 46 -- the baron shall have the wardship of the abbey` IS
    IF   `the person is a baron`
     AND `he founded the abbey`
    THEN IF   (   `he holds charters from the kings of England concerning the abbey`
               OR `he has long-continued possession of the abbey`)
          AND `the abbey is vacant`
         THEN Entitled
         -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "who have founded abbeys, concerning which they hold charters ... when vacant" states who has the wardship; it says nothing of a baron with neither charters nor possession, or of an abbey not vacant (F64c).
         ELSE `Entitlement not covered`
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text does not speak to a case outside the facts this rule names.
    ELSE `Entitlement not covered`

§§ `Chapter 47 -- forests and river-banks of the King's time`

-- "forthwith" fixes no period, so no deadline is encoded (fork F47).
-- src:101 | All forests that have been made such in our time shall forthwith be disafforested;
@ref Chapter 47 clause 47.1 (src:101)
GIVEN `the forest was made a forest in the King's time` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN Obligation
DECIDE `ch 47 -- the forest is to be disafforested` IS
    IF `the forest was made a forest in the King's time`
    THEN Obliged
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text does not speak to a case outside the facts this rule names.
    ELSE `Obligation not covered`

-- src:101 | and a similar course shall be followed with regard to river-banks that have been placed “in defence” by us in our time.
@ref Chapter 47 clause 47.2 (src:101)
GIVEN `the river-bank was placed in defence by the King in his time` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN Obligation
DECIDE `ch 47 -- the river-bank is to be released from defence` IS
    IF `the river-bank was placed in defence by the King in his time`
    THEN Obliged
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text does not speak to a case outside the facts this rule names.
    ELSE `Obligation not covered`

§§ `Chapter 48 -- evil customs of the forest`

-- The proviso "provided always that we previously have intimation thereof, or our justiciar, if we
-- should not be in England" is read as a condition of both the inquiry and the abolition (fork F48a).
-- src:103 | All evil customs connected with forests and warrens, foresters and warreners, sheriffs and their officers, river-banks and their wardens, shall immediately be inquired into in each county by twelve sworn knights of the same county chosen by the honest men of the same county,
-- src:103 | provided always that we previously have intimation thereof, or our justiciar, if we should not be in England.
@ref Chapter 48 clause 48.1 and 48.3 (src:103)
GIVEN `the custom is an evil custom connected with forests and warrens, foresters and warreners, sheriffs and their officers, river-banks and their wardens` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the King, or his justiciar if the King is not in England, has previously had intimation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN Obligation
DECIDE `ch 48 -- the evil custom is to be inquired into` IS
    IF `the custom is an evil custom connected with forests and warrens, foresters and warreners, sheriffs and their officers, river-banks and their wardens`
    THEN IF `the King, or his justiciar if the King is not in England, has previously had intimation`
         THEN Obliged
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "provided always that we previously have intimation thereof".
         ELSE `Not obliged`
    -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: if, provided because "provided" and "if" condition the inquiry on intimation and have their own branch; this branch is reached by a custom that is not one the sentence names).
    ELSE `Obligation not covered`

-- src:103 | All evil customs connected with forests and warrens, foresters and warreners, sheriffs and their officers, river-banks and their wardens, shall immediately be inquired into in each county by twelve sworn knights of the same county chosen by the honest men of the same county,
@ref Chapter 48 clause 48.1 (src:103)
GIVEN `the number of knights` IS A NUMBER
      `they are sworn` IS A BOOLEAN
      `they are knights of the same county` IS A BOOLEAN
      `they were chosen by the honest men of the same county` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ch 48 -- the inquest is constituted as the text requires` IS
        `the number of knights` EQUALS 12
    AND `they are sworn`
    AND `they are knights of the same county`
    AND `they were chosen by the honest men of the same county`

-- "within forty days of the said inquest" (fork F48b: the day of the inquest is not counted, and
-- the fortieth day after it is the last).
-- src:103 | and shall, within forty days of the said inquest, be utterly abolished, so as never to be restored,
@ref Chapter 48 clause 48.2 (src:103)
GIVEN `the day of the inquest` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `ch 48 -- the last day for abolishing the evil custom` IS
    Date (Day `the day of the inquest` PLUS 40)

-- src:103 | and shall, within forty days of the said inquest, be utterly abolished, so as never to be restored,
-- src:103 | provided always that we previously have intimation thereof, or our justiciar, if we should not be in England.
@ref Chapter 48 clause 48.2-48.3 (src:103)
GIVEN `the custom is an evil custom connected with forests and warrens, foresters and warreners, sheriffs and their officers, river-banks and their wardens` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the King, or his justiciar if the King is not in England, has previously had intimation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN Obligation
DECIDE `ch 48 -- the evil custom is to be utterly abolished` IS
    IF `the custom is an evil custom connected with forests and warrens, foresters and warreners, sheriffs and their officers, river-banks and their wardens`
    THEN IF `the King, or his justiciar if the King is not in England, has previously had intimation`
         THEN Obliged
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "provided always that we previously have intimation thereof".
         ELSE `Not obliged`
    -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: if, provided because "provided" and "if" condition the abolition on intimation and have their own branch; this branch is reached by a custom that is not one the sentence names).
    ELSE `Obligation not covered`

-- src:103 | and shall, within forty days of the said inquest, be utterly abolished, so as never to be restored,
@ref Chapter 48 clause 48.2 (src:103)
GIVEN `the day of the inquest` IS A DATE
      `the day of abolition` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
